2014-09-16 20:53 GMT+04:00 Bob LeChevalier, President and Founder - LLG <
lojbab@lojban.org>:

> On 9/15/2014 11:14 AM, 'John E Clifford' via lojban wrote:
>
>> Yes, the proliferation problem is a serious one.  There are a half a
>> dozen logical languages (in the present sense) around in various stages
>> of development (mostly early) and activity (mostly slight).  This new
>> enterprise seems to be driven less by the old "perfection being the
>> enemy of the good" bugbear and more by frustration with bureaucratic
>> inertia (though, interestingly, the first step in the project is to set
>> up a new bureaucracy).  Personally, though I am perfection-driven, I
>> have decided that it is a better use of time and talent (a lot of the
>> one, a little of the other) to work with a nearly completed project in
>> the hope that it will mange to get completed into a product that is good
>> enough (does all the crucial things, even if in sloppy, inefficient,
>> even ugly, ways).
>>
>
Provided both languages have a formal syntax and a clearly defined
connection between the two dictionaries it won't matter a lot what language
will be spoken since the machine translation will be 100% precise under
such circumstances.


> I think you are correct.  I can imagine a successor to Lojban emerging in
> the future, perhaps being developed by people using Lojban rather than
> English as the medium of communication, which incidentally might reduce the
> likelihood of dominance by English native speakers.
>
> But I won't be involved in such a successor effort.  And the inherent
> competition between artificial languages for the limited pool of interested
> people and their time means that I cannot wish such an effort to be
> successful so long as Lojban has a chance to be.
>
> lojbab
